# ADR-019: API Versioning Strategy

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| ADR | 019 |
| Title | API Versioning Strategy |
| Status | Accepted |
| Date | 2026-07-27 |
| Decision Makers | R-zension Engineering Team |

---

# Context

The R-zension platform exposes APIs to multiple clients, including

- React Web Application
- Future Mobile Applications
- Administrative Portal
- Oracle AI Service
- Third-party Integrations
- Future Public APIs

As the platform evolves, APIs will gain new features, improve existing endpoints, and occasionally introduce breaking changes.

A consistent versioning strategy is required to ensure backward compatibility while allowing continuous platform evolution.

---

# Decision

R-zension will use **URI-based API Versioning**.

Every public API endpoint will include an explicit version identifier.

Example

```text
/api/v1/
```

Major breaking changes require a new API version.

Minor, backward-compatible enhancements remain within the same version.

---

# Objectives

The API versioning strategy should

- Preserve backward compatibility
- Minimize client disruption
- Simplify maintenance
- Support gradual migrations
- Enable long-term evolution
- Maintain predictable APIs

---

# API Structure

Example

```text
/api/v1/auth

/api/v1/users

/api/v1/goals

/api/v1/leaderboard

/api/v1/rankings

/api/v1/oracle

/api/v1/analytics
```

Future versions

```text
/api/v2/

/api/v3/
```

---

# Version Scope

Versioning applies to

- Public REST APIs
- External integrations
- Mobile APIs
- Future partner APIs

Internal service communication may evolve independently where appropriate.

---

# Breaking Changes

Require a new version when

- Endpoint removal
- Request schema changes
- Response schema changes
- Authentication changes
- Authorization behavior changes
- Resource model redesign

---

# Non-Breaking Changes

Remain in the same version

Examples

- New optional fields
- Performance improvements
- Bug fixes
- Internal optimizations
- Additional endpoints
- Documentation improvements

---

# Response Headers

Recommended response headers

```text
API-Version: v1

Deprecation: false
```

Future deprecation notices may include sunset information.

---

# Deprecation Policy

When introducing a new version

1. Announce the change.
2. Maintain the previous version during the support period.
3. Publish migration documentation.
4. Remove the deprecated version after the announced sunset date.

Clients should receive sufficient notice before version retirement.

---

# Endpoint Stability

Within a major version

- Request formats remain stable.
- Response formats remain stable.
- Existing behavior remains compatible.

Breaking changes are prohibited within the same major version.

---

# Documentation

Every API version should have independent documentation.

Example

```text
API v1 Documentation

API v2 Documentation
```

Documentation must clearly identify

- Added endpoints
- Deprecated endpoints
- Removed endpoints
- Migration guidance

---

# OpenAPI Specification

Each version maintains its own OpenAPI specification.

Example

```text
openapi-v1.yaml

openapi-v2.yaml
```

Specifications should be generated from the source code whenever possible.

---

# Oracle API

Oracle follows the same versioning strategy.

Examples

```text
/api/v1/oracle/chat

/api/v1/oracle/explain-score

/api/v1/oracle/recommendations
```

AI prompt versions are managed independently of API versions.

---

# Error Responses

Error response structures remain stable within a major version.

Example

```json id="g8o3l1"
{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "code": "RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND",
    "message": "Requested resource could not be found.",
    "requestId": "REQ-91FA22"
  }
}
```

---

# Authentication

Authentication mechanisms remain consistent within a major version.

Major authentication redesigns require

```text
v2

v3
```

rather than changing existing behavior.

---

# Testing

Each API version requires

- Unit Tests
- Integration Tests
- Contract Tests
- Regression Tests

Older supported versions remain part of the automated test suite.

---

# Monitoring

Track

- Requests by API version
- Error rate by version
- Deprecated endpoint usage
- Migration progress
- Version adoption

These metrics inform deprecation planning.

---

# Security

Security patches should be applied to all supported API versions.

Unsupported versions no longer receive updates.

---

# Performance Goals

| Metric | Target |
|---------|---------|
| Version Routing | < 5 ms |
| API Availability | > 99.9% |
| Request Latency | No measurable overhead |

Versioning should not introduce significant runtime cost.

---

# Alternatives Considered

## No Versioning

Pros

- Simple implementation

Cons

- Frequent client breakage
- Difficult maintenance
- Poor long-term evolution

Decision

Rejected.

---

## Header-Based Versioning

Pros

- Clean URLs
- Flexible

Cons

- Harder debugging
- Less visible
- Less intuitive for API consumers

Decision

Rejected.

---

## URI-Based Versioning

Pros

- Clear
- Explicit
- Easy documentation
- Easy routing
- Widely adopted

Decision

Accepted.

---

# Consequences

Positive

- Stable APIs
- Easier client upgrades
- Predictable evolution
- Improved documentation
- Better developer experience

Negative

- Multiple versions require maintenance
- Documentation duplication
- Larger automated test surface

---

# Future Evolution

Future enhancements include

- API gateway integration
- GraphQL gateway
- Automatic version negotiation
- API lifecycle management
- Partner-specific APIs
- Public developer portal
- SDK generation
- API analytics dashboards

---

# Decision Summary

R-zension adopts URI-based API versioning to provide stable, backward-compatible interfaces while supporting continuous platform evolution. Major changes are introduced through new API versions, ensuring predictable behavior, simpler migrations, and an excellent developer experience.

